      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Blend the tomatoes, green chilli, garlic, onion, lime juice, and coriander with a pinch of salt until smooth. This is your green salsa."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat oil in a frying pan over medium heat. Pour in the salsa and fry for 8 minutes, stirring often, until it darkens slightly and thickens."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Warm each tortilla in a dry pan for 20 seconds per side until just pliable."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spoon a little salsa into a baking dish to coat the base. Fill each tortilla with shredded chicken, roll tightly, and place seam-side down in the dish."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the remaining salsa over the rolled tortillas. Drizzle the yoghurt on top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bake at 200C for 20 minutes until the edges are bubbling and the top has a little colour. Serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Tortillas crack when rolling",
              "cause": "They were not warmed enough to become pliable.",
              "fix": "Heat each tortilla an extra 10 seconds per side in the dry pan. They should feel soft and flexible."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Salsa tastes flat",
              "cause": "Not enough acid or the tomatoes were under-ripe.",
              "fix": "Add another squeeze of lime and a pinch of salt. Frying the salsa longer also deepens the flavour."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Filling falls out during baking",
              "cause": "Enchiladas were not placed seam-side down.",
              "fix": "Always lay rolled enchiladas seam-side down so the weight of the sauce holds them closed."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Enchiladas are eaten across Mexico but verdes are most associated with central and southern states where tomatillos grow wild. The green sauce has a sharper, fresher character than the red mole-based version. Families in Mexico City often make enchiladas verdes for Sunday lunch, finishing the dish with crumbled fresh cheese and a few drops of cream. The dish moves fast from pan to table, so everyone eats it piping hot."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
